From b274a78b4cb614f71ca2cb77bbafcb34de6a0b95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:37:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 58/65] MAS local: add the modavd_ime host input method pipe

A goldfish pipe service for the ModAVD host input method channel,
protocol v1. The guest input method (org.modavd.ime) connects to
"modavd_ime" and sends a handshake ('V', u32 version), the focused editor
('F': u8 active, u32 inputType, u32 imeOptions) and its cursor rectangle
in display pixels ('R': 4 x i32). The host answers the handshake and
sends committed text ('C'), composing text ('P': i32 cursor + text), the
end of composing ('E'), key presses ('K': u32 keycode, u32 metaState,
u8 action) and deletions around the cursor ('D': i32 before, i32 after).
A frame is a little-endian u32 payload length, 1 to 65536, and the
payload, whose first byte is the type; text is UTF-8.

Only the latest connection counts: a new one closes the previous one,
which is kept until the guest closes it too. A first frame that is not
'V', a bad length, an unknown type or missing fields close the
connection. Like the clipboard pipe, it is not saved in snapshots;
loading one closes it and the guest reconnects. Host data waiting for a
guest that stopped reading is capped at 1 MB.

The window and the console use the thread-safe static API: send*(),
getState() and state callbacks, which run on the device thread with no
pipe lock held. A wake queued by a host thread just before the guest
closes the pipe is cancelled once the pipe is marked closed, so it
cannot reach the freed hw pipe.

Test: a guest shell script talking to /dev/goldfish_pipe_dprctd: the
      handshake reply, status after F and R, the bytes of every host
      frame, eight kinds of malformed frame (each closes only that
      connection) and a second connection replacing the first.
Change-Id: Ib44e73b0a24061b71a4045e8e9273817bdb608fe
